## Fix:
The error happens because local variables in Ruby are scoped to the context where they are defined. Your x variable inside the counter method is completely isolated from the x you defined outside.
## Why it failed

* x = 10 creates a local variable in the top-level main scope.
* def counter starts a brand new, isolated local scope. It cannot see x from the outside.
* x += 1 expands to x = x + 1. Because x does not exist inside the method yet, Ruby treats the right-hand x as an uninitialized local variable, which evaluates to nil. You cannot add 1 to nil.

------------------------------
## How to fix it
To make a working closure in Ruby, define the method using a block (like define_method) or pass x into the method as an argument.
## Option 1: Use define_method (Preserves the closure)
Using define_method keeps the current scope alive, allowing the Proc to see and modify x.

x = 10
# define_method captures the surrounding local variables
define_method(:counter) do
  Proc.new { x += 1 }end

c = counter
puts c.call # => 11
puts c.call # => 12
puts x      # => 12 (the original x is modified)

## Option 2: Pass x as a parameter
If you want to keep using standard def syntax, pass the starting value into the method.

def counter(start_value)
  Proc.new { start_value += 1 }end

c = counter(10)
puts c.call # => 11
puts c.call # => 12

